    "narrative": "The Austin Waves lineup for June 1, 2026, is anchored by **Kyle Tucker** (74.70) and **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (71.51), both of whom benefit significantly from favorable matchups against mediocre opposing starters. **Kyle Tucker** remains our premier offensive engine, enjoying an elite score driven by a 13% boost from his matchup against Eduardo Rodriguez, while **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** capitalizes on a strong platoon advantage against Sandy Alcantara. Though **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** carries a minor BvP tax due to limited historical success, his dynamic platoon splits and premium batting order placement outweigh the risk, making him a must-start for offensive production. **Andy Pages** (69.40) also earns a high-confidence start, as he thrives in the same high-run-environment setup against Rodriguez.\n\nIn terms of lineup construction, **Nasim Nunez** (57.11) represents an intriguing tactical play; while his order position is lower than ideal, he earns a meaningful bump due to his elite sprint speed and a high-probability opportunity to exploit the slow delivery of Alcantara on the basepaths\u2014a critical factor given our 5x5 scarcity constraints. The closest decision in the final lineup selection involved **Liam Hicks** (51.05) and **Miguel Vargas** (42.56). **Miguel Vargas** barely made the cut as a corner infield option, suppressed by a difficult matchup against Joe Ryan, but his projection remained marginally safer than our deeper bench alternatives. We are currently navigating the absences of **Luis Robert Jr.**, who remains sidelined, and rest days for core pieces like **Gunnar Henderson**, **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.**, and **Jackson Merrill**, which has forced us to lean into the depth provided by **Josh Jung** (54.71) and **Sam Antonacci** (48.32). \n\nRisks for today\u2019s slate are centered primarily on the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Kyle Tucker** and **Andy Pages**. While we are modeling both under the assumption of favorable batting order placement, any shift down the lineup could trigger a re-evaluation of their daily efficiency scores. Additionally, while we are confident in the projected output of our starters, we remain vigilant regarding the high-floor, high-risk nature of facing pitchers like Joe Ryan, who continues to suppress the raw scores of both **Miguel Vargas** and **Sam Antonacci**. We have elected to leave bench depth like **Jordan Westburg**, **Jorge Polanco**, **Drake Baldwin**, **Josh Lowe**, **Jacob Wilson**, **Heliot Ramos**, **Miguel Andujar**, **Coby Mayo**, **Nolan Schanuel**, **Dominic Smith**, and **Brayan Rocchio** in reserve to preserve our game caps for more favorable future conditions.",
